# What are the differences between special food in Vietnam and other countries?
I think one of the main differences lies in the ingredients and cooking styles. Vietnamese special dishes often emphasize fresh ingredients, herbs, and a balance of flavors, including sweet, sour, salty, and spicy tastes.

In addition, many traditional Vietnamese foods are closely associated with cultural festivals and family gatherings. For example, dishes like banh chung are an important part of Tet celebrations. In contrast, special foods in other countries may reflect different traditions, local ingredients, or historical influences.

Overall, while every country has its own unique cuisine, Vietnamese food is often considered lighter and fresher than many Western dishes.

# Is there any food in your country that is eaten at special times or on special occasions?
Yes, absolutely. Food plays an important role in many Vietnamese festivals and celebrations.

One of the best examples is Bánh Chưng, which is traditionally prepared and eaten during Tet. It symbolizes gratitude to ancestors and reflects the importance of family in Vietnamese culture. Families often gather together to make these cakes, which is a tradition in itself.

Another example is mooncakes, which are closely linked to the Mid-Autumn Festival. People often give them as gifts to relatives, friends, or business partners as a way of expressing appreciation and good wishes.

Overall, I would say that many traditional foods in Vietnam have strong cultural and symbolic value. They are not just meals but also an important part of our identity and heritage.

# Why are some people willing to spend a lot of money on meals on special days?
I think people are willing to spend more money on meals during special occasions because these events are often meaningful and don't happen very often. Celebrations such as birthdays, weddings, or anniversaries are important milestones, so people want to make them memorable.

Another reason is that sharing a good meal is a way of showing love and appreciation to family members and friends. Many people choose to dine at a nice restaurant or order high-quality food to create a special atmosphere and enjoy the occasion together.

Overall, I think people are not just paying for the food itself, but also for the experience and the opportunity to create lasting memories with their loved ones.

# Do you think it’s good to communicate when eating with your family?
Yes, definitely. I think mealtimes are one of the best opportunities for family members to communicate and spend quality time together. Since everyone is usually busy with work, school, or other responsibilities, having a meal together gives them a chance to catch up on each other's lives and share their experiences.

In addition, conversations during meals can help strengthen family bonds and create a warm, welcoming atmosphere at home. Family members can support one another, discuss problems, or simply enjoy each other's company. In my opinion, these small daily interactions play an important role in maintaining close relationships.

Overall, I believe eating and talking together is a simple but effective way to keep families connected.

# In your country, do people nowadays cook at home as frequently as people did in the past?
No, I don't think so. Compared to the past, people nowadays tend to cook at home less often, mainly because modern lifestyles have become much busier. Many people work long hours and may not have enough time or energy to prepare meals after a tiring day.

Another reason is the growing popularity of food delivery services and restaurants. With just a few taps on a smartphone, people can order a wide variety of meals and have them delivered to their doorstep, which is both convenient and time-saving.

That being said, home cooking is still an important part of Vietnamese culture. Many families continue to cook together on weekends or during special occasions such as Tet, when traditional dishes are prepared and shared among family members.

Overall, while home cooking may be less common than it was in the past, it still plays a significant role in family life and cultural traditions.

# What do you think of people using their mobile phones during a meal?
I think it depends on the situation. If someone quickly checks an important message or answers an urgent phone call, that's completely understandable. However, if people spend most of the meal scrolling through social media or watching videos, it can be quite impolite.

One of the main purposes of sharing a meal is to spend time with others and have meaningful conversations. When people are constantly looking at their phones, they may miss opportunities to connect with family members or friends. It can also make the people around them feel ignored or unappreciated.

That being said, mobile phones have become an essential part of modern life, so it's unrealistic to expect people to avoid them completely. In my opinion, the key is to use them in moderation and be mindful of the people you're dining with.

# More and more people are unwilling to cook. Why is this happening?
I think there are several reasons for this trend. First of all, modern life has become increasingly fast-paced, and many people have demanding work or study schedules. After a long day, they often prefer to order food rather than spend time shopping for ingredients and cooking.

Another major factor is the rise of food delivery services. Nowadays, people can choose from a wide variety of meals and have them delivered to their homes within a short period of time. This convenience makes eating out or ordering food much more attractive than preparing meals from scratch.

In addition, not everyone has the necessary cooking skills or interest in cooking. Younger generations, in particular, may rely more on restaurants and ready-made meals because they were not taught how to cook regularly at home.

Overall, I think the combination of busy lifestyles, convenience, and changing habits has made home cooking less popular than it used to be.
